{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghbrT6hjft6vEWe5rLFc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Permanganate cannot be analysed with ligand-field theory, so I can't give Dq or B for it.\n\n- **The metal is d⁰.** Mn in MnO₄⁻ is Mn(VII), with configuration [Ar] 3d⁰. The ligand-field model gives Dq and Racah B from d–d term splittings. A d⁰ ion has no d electrons, so it has no d–d transitions and no terms to split.\n- **The observed bands are charge-transfer.** The intense bands at 17 570, 31 560 and 38 320 cm⁻¹ (ε ≈ 1 500–2 900 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹) are O→Mn ligand-to-metal charge-transfer transitions. They are Laporte-allowed, which explains their intensity. Their energies depend on the Mn–O electronegativity and orbital overlap, not on a Tanabe–Sugano d–d energy matrix.\n- **Fitting anyway would be meaningless.** Forcing a d–d model onto these bands would produce numbers with no physical meaning for Dq or B. The supported configurations are d² to d⁸ only, so no tool-based fit applies here.\n\nIf you have a d–d spectrum of a reduced or d¹–d⁹ manganese species, such as Mn(II) d⁵ or Mn(III) d⁴, I can fit Dq and B for that instead.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"MnO4- is d0, so its bands are O→Mn charge-transfer transitions, not d-d transitions, and Dq and B are undefined in the ligand-field model.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8059,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8059,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cbee376-2267-46f8-a609-e66de0a60e33","uuid":"d3598eef-3b2b-43e1-9f94-442fb6176b3c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T08:50:45.927Z